WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Okay, in this video we're going to look at the last stage of cellular respiration, the electron transport chain.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:14.000
Remember, NADH and FADH2 picked up high-energy electrons during glycolysis and the Krebs cycle. Now they drop those electrons off at the inner mitochondrial membrane.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:23.000
The electrons get passed from one protein to the next down the chain, kind of like a ball being passed down a line of people. Each time, they lose a little energy.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:33.000
The proteins use that energy to pump hydrogen ions, H plus, across the inner membrane, so hydrogen ions build up in the space between the two membranes.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:43.000
Then the hydrogen ions flow back across the membrane through a protein called ATP synthase. ATP synthase spins like a little turbine, and as it spins it makes ATP from ADP and phosphate.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:53.000
So what happens to the electrons at the end of the chain? This is where oxygen comes in. Oxygen is the final electron acceptor.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:03.000
At the end of the chain, oxygen picks up the electrons and combines with hydrogen ions to form water. That's the water in the equation for cellular respiration.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:12.000
And that's why you need to breathe oxygen. Without oxygen to accept the electrons, the whole chain backs up and stops, and the cell can't make most of its ATP.
